# A sidebar row clicked once opens a PREVIEW tab; a second click keeps it.
#
# The point of preview mode is that browsing a list does not leave a tab behind
# per glance, so the assertions are about SLOTS: the preview marker and the
# active tab both sit at slot 0 (x=284) while three different threads are
# looked at in turn, and only once a tab has been kept does the next glance get
# a slot of its own beside it (x=508). Tabs are 220 wide on a 4px pitch, inset
# 4px from the pane edge, so slot 1 starts at 284+224.
#
# Rows are clicked by coordinate because the harness cannot name one row of a
# list (afterhours_gaps.md #55): the band starts at y=380 with 24px rows, so
# y=386 / y=410 / y=434 are the first three threads.
#
# settings: {"window_width":1100,"window_height":760,"open_tabs":[],"active_tab":"","theme":"dark"}

mouse_move 650 400
wait_frames 10

# One click: a preview tab, marked as temporary, and it is the current tab.
click 140 386
wait_frames 8
assert_ui tab_preview x=288
assert_ui tab_active x=284

# A second thread REUSES that tab rather than opening another — the marker and
# the accent are both still on slot 0, so there is exactly one tab and it is
# still a preview.
click 140 410
wait_frames 8
assert_ui tab_preview x=288
assert_ui tab_active x=284

# Clicking the tab you are already reading is the second look, and the second
# look keeps it. The next glance can no longer take that slot: it opens beside
# the kept tab instead.
click_ui tab_label
wait_frames 8
click 140 434
wait_frames 8
assert_ui tab_preview x=512
assert_ui tab_active x=508
